# Part Eight — Pakistani Firsts and Records

> Women "firsts" in Pakistan's politics, state and public life — a favourite source of MCQs (2016, 2018, 2019, 2024).

::: table | Pakistani women firsts.
@widths 1.6,4.4,8
@font 9.5
Year | Person | First
1947 | Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz and Begum Shaista Ikramullah | The two women members of the first Constituent Assembly (MCQs 2016, 2024)
1949 | Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan | Founded APWA; later first woman governor (Sindh, 1973)
1965 | Fatima Jinnah | First woman to contest a presidential election (against Ayub Khan)
1988 | Benazir Bhutto | First woman prime minister — of Pakistan and of any Muslim-majority country (MCQ 2018)
1989 | First Women Bank | First bank run by and for women
1994 | Women's police stations | First police stations staffed by women
2008 | Dr Fahmida Mirza | First woman Speaker of the National Assembly (MCQs 2016, 2019)
2011 | Hina Rabbani Khar | First woman foreign minister
2012 | Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy | First Pakistani to win an Oscar (*Saving Face*)
2014 | Malala Yousafzai | Youngest Nobel laureate (aged 17) — first Pakistani woman Nobel laureate
2017 | Tehmina Janjua | First woman foreign secretary (MCQ 2018)
2020 | Lt Gen Nigar Johar | First woman lieutenant general
2022 | Justice Ayesha Malik | First woman judge of the Supreme Court
2024 | Maryam Nawaz | First woman chief minister (Punjab)
:::

::: table | Records and "only" facts.
@widths 6,8
@font 9.5
Fact | Detail
Most women on general NA seats in recent elections | 11 in 2024 (8 in 2018)
Worst Global Gender Gap rank | 148th of 148 (2025) — last
Best recent Global Gender Gap gain | 2026: up to 143rd of 145, 59.5% — strongest gain in a decade
First provincial domestic-violence law | Sindh (2013)
First province to set the marriage age at 18 | Sindh (2013); Islamabad followed (2025)
First one-stop Violence against Women Centre | Multan (2017)
Lowest provincial female LFPR | Balochistan, 14.5% (2024–25)
Highest provincial female LFPR | Punjab, 33.8% (2024–25)
:::
